# 13–14px Desktop Baseline & 0.9em Code

The fundamental purpose of typography in productivity software is to sustain high-density, low-fatigue information intake across hours of continuous work.

## Productivity Tools vs Marketing Pages

- **Marketing Sites / Blogs**: Prefer larger type (16px–20px) with loose leading to capture passive attention and encourage rapid scrolling.
- **Desktop Productivity Tools (IDEs, Consoles, CoreBox)**: Must anchor body type between 13px–14px. Oversized type dilutes information density, forcing constant eye travel that violates the Gestalt Law of Proximity and accelerates visual exhaustion.

## The 13–14px Optical Scanning Baseline

The human foveal vision window covers approximately 2 degrees. At typical desktop viewing distances (50cm–70cm), 13px–14px allows full word units to land inside the fovea, optimizing scanning velocity:

- **Controls & Data Rows (13px–14px)**: Input field values, table cells, button labels.
- **Secondary Copy (12px)**: Helper copy, empty state notes, shortcut badges.
- **Micro-labels (10px–11px)**: All-caps tags or miniature badges.
- **Headings (16px–18px)**: Reserved strictly for card and module titles; prose never exceeds 14px.

## Monospace X-Height Inflation Mechanics

Technical documentation routinely mixes inline code (`<code>`) into body copy.
Sans-serif typefaces (e.g. Inter, SF Pro) and monospace code faces (e.g. JetBrains Mono, SF Mono) carry fundamentally divergent font metrics:
To maximize legibility in code editors, monospace typefaces inflate letter counters—their **x-height runs 12%–18% taller than equivalent sans-serif type**!

## Optical Alignment: Scaling Inline Code to 0.9em

If inline code inherits `font-size: 1em`:
- Monospace tokens stick out awkwardly like unharmonized patches.
- Inflated height distorts uniform line-height rhythms.

TuffEx enforces the standard:

:::TuffCodeBlock{lang="scss"}
---
code: |
  .tx-prose code,
  .tx-inline-code {
    font-family: var(--tx-font-family-mono);
    // Scaled to 0.9em to compensate for monospace x-height inflation
    font-size: 0.9em;
    padding: 0.15em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background-color: var(--tx-fill-color-light);
    vertical-align: baseline;
  }
---
:::
At 0.9em, monospace stems align with adjacent sans-serif glyphs, preserving rhythmic integrity across mixed technical prose.
